# catalog/lane-run-inputs.d/watchdog-responder.rw — the per-project files one
# `bin/watchdog-responder.sh $p` run writes. A write-back may carry these only.
# The ro snapshot is these plus watchdog-responder.ro. The inventory, with
# file:line for each entry, is in
# docs/notes/2026-10-14-BH-6720-llm-alert-lanes-offhub.md.
#
# NOT CARRIED. A project run also writes these fleet-wide files, and they
# block a flip of watchdog-responder to Fly:
#   state/responder/handled.json (one JSON keyed <project>:<key>, rewritten
#     under a lock, bin/watchdog-responder.sh:134-141,239,257,285,911-916;
#     not a project-keyed TSV, so @row does not fit)
#   logs/responder.log (every project run appends to one log,
#     bin/watchdog-responder.sh:74; the lane log that rides back is
#     logs/watchdog-responder.log)
#   logs/ALERTS.log, flags/lane-caps-degraded-*, logs/lane-caps.log,
#   logs/model-route.log, logs/agent-rules.log, logs/cost.csv
# The prompt launches /root/harness/bin/run-build.sh by absolute path
# (bin/watchdog-responder.sh:364), and the board token comes from
# bin/internal-token-for.sh (env/$p.env, bin/watchdog-responder.sh:405,523),
# a second per-project credential a lane machine does not have.
# Secrets, locks and machine-local caches are never carried: env/*, locks/*,
# state/gh-*, state/settings-cache.json, state/lane-gate/*.
#
# stale-history.json, stale-owed.json, .wedgekeys
# (bin/watchdog-responder.sh:133,219-225,253-267,906-907)
state/watchdog-responder/$p/**
# bin/watchdog-responder.sh:784,790-810,816-851
logs/responder-decide.$p.txt
logs/responder-run.$p.txt
